\textbf{Response 1A}

\textbf{Prediction before Output 1.1:} Before seeing the results, I
expected Period-routed Ridge to perform best because it can use a
different linear model for different periods. I expected Raw Attention
to do reasonably well on the established stations, but I was less
confident about its performance on the held-out Station 4. I expected
the two non-routed ridge models to perform worse because they cannot
directly account for the different product speeds.

\textbf{Results and revised ordering:} The results supported the first
part of this prediction. On the established-station validation split
(Stations 1--3), the ranking was \textbf{Period-routed Ridge (RMSE
0.166) \textgreater{} Raw Attention (0.428) \textgreater{} Shared Ridge
(0.767) ≈ Sensor-specific Ridge (0.768)}. On the held-out Station 4
validation split, the ranking was \textbf{Period-routed Ridge (0.173)
\textgreater{} Raw Attention (0.632) \textgreater{} Shared Ridge
(0.876)}. Sensor-specific Ridge was not available for Station 4 because
that station was held out during fitting.

Period-routed Ridge was much better than Raw Attention. On the
established validation split, its RMSE was about 61\% lower than Raw
Attention, and on the held-out validation split it was about 73\% lower.
The improvement was also consistent across the three products. For
established stations, Period-routed Ridge had RMSEs of 0.181, 0.164, and
0.152 for Products A, B, and C, compared with 0.392, 0.470, and 0.420
for Raw Attention.

This changed my initial expectation that the more flexible attention
model might be competitive with period routing. The results suggest that
explicitly using the known period information was more useful than
relying on attention to learn the different temporal patterns.

\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=3.69167in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image1.png}

Figure 1.2: Validation RMSE comparison across the four forecasting
models for Station 1, Products A and C.

\textbf{Response 1B}

Output 1.2 shows the problem with using one fixed ridge matrix. For
Station 1, Product A has an actual period of about 16.1 samples, while
Product C has a period of about 30.1 samples. The same matrix has to use
the same temporal weights for both products. Therefore, weights that are
useful for one pace may correspond to a different phase of the pattern
at another pace. The model has to compromise, which can affect both the
phase and shape of the forecast.

For example, on Station 1, Period-routed Ridge had RMSEs of 0.232 for
Product A and 0.150 for Product C, while Shared Ridge had RMSEs of 0.779
and 0.448.

Attention is different because its weights depend on the input context.
The same learned WQW\_Q, WKW\_K, and WVW\_V matrices are used, but the
queries and keys produced from different contexts can be different. This
changes the attention scores and therefore the final attention weights.

In Output 1.3, the mean absolute difference between the attention
weights for Station 1 Products A and C was 0.012, with a maximum
difference of 0.415. A ridge model, in contrast, keeps fixed
coefficients for a given routing choice. This shows that attention
changes its mixing based on the context; it does not show that attention
recovered the actual physical period.

\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=1.85417in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image2.png}

Figure 1.3: Difference in attention weights between Station 1 contexts
for Product A and Product C.

\hypertarget{response-2}{%
\subsubsection{Response 2}\label{response-2}}

The selected moving-average width was \textbf{41}. In Output 2.1,
increasing the width changed how much of the signal was treated as
trend. The residual standard deviation increased from 0.578 at width 17
to 1.572 at width 41, while the trend range stayed around 8.0--8.8.

\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=1.68194in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image3.png}

Figure 2.1: Effect of moving-average decomposition width on residual
variation and trend range.

The diagnostic in Output 2.2 selected width 41 because it recovered the
oracle period most accurately: \textbf{99.8\% of the periods were within
one sample}, compared with 68.7\% for the raw signal.

\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=2.82708in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image4.png}

Figure 2.2: Oracle period-recovery accuracy for the raw signal and
different moving-average widths.

The decomposition also improved the forecasting results. On the
established-station validation split (Stations 1--3), Raw Attention had
RMSE \textbf{0.428} and MSE \textbf{0.184}, while Attention +
decomposition had RMSE \textbf{0.308} and MSE \textbf{0.095}. The
improvement was also seen on the held-out Station 4 validation split,
where RMSE decreased from \textbf{0.632 to 0.457}. The product-level
results also improved for A, B, and C.

\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=3.69167in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image5.png}

Figure 2.3: Validation performance of Raw Attention and Attention +
Decomposition across Products A, B, and C.

A moving average makes sense here because the data contains a slower
changing component as well as repeated patterns. It gives the model a
separate trend component instead of making attention learn both types of
variation from the same input. The oracle diagnostic is useful for
choosing the width because it gives a direct way to check whether the
decomposition is preserving the known periodic structure. It is only
used as an offline diagnostic, not as information given to the
forecasting model.

One limitation is that a moving average may not work well with sudden
changes or isolated events. A sharp transition can be spread across both
the trend and residual components. In that case, a different
decomposition method, such as a learned or more local decomposition,
could be more suitable.

\hypertarget{response-3}{%
\subsubsection{Response 3}\label{response-3}}

The worked example in Output 3.1 confirmed the direction used for the
delays. For delays 0--3, the FFT-based scores matched the direct
calculation exactly: \textbf{4, -4, 4, -4}. The aggregation example also
confirmed that a delay of dd uses the value at (t−d)(t-d) modulo the
context length.

For Station 2, Product A, Output 3.2 showed strong recurrence at delays
close to the product period and its multiple. The expected period was
about \textbf{16.1 samples}, and the nearest delay was 16 with a
residual-signal correlation of \textbf{0.896}. At twice the period, the
expected delay was about 32 samples and the correlation was
\textbf{0.897}. This indicates that both the main period and its
multiple are useful delays.

\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=1.66111in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image6.png}

Figure 3.2: Residual-signal correlation at delays corresponding to the
dominant period and its multiple for Station 2, Product A.

Decomposing the signal first makes this evidence easier to see because
the slower changing component has been removed. The remaining signal
contains more of the repeated pattern, so delay mixing can focus on past
values that occur at similar phases.

This approach can fail when the signal does not have stable recurrence.
For example, an aperiodic event, a rapidly changing period, or a
one-time transient would not be well represented by a small set of fixed
delays. Also, the correlations in Output 3.2 are \textbf{signal-level
diagnostics}, not learned model attention or delay scores.

\hypertarget{response-4}{%
\subsubsection{Response 4}\label{response-4}}

\textbf{Prediction before Output 4.1.} I expected decomposition to help
most when the slow component was strong because separating it should
make the repeated pattern easier to model. I also expected delay mixing
to help because the data has clear periodic recurrence.

On the established-station validation split (Stations 1--3), the RMSEs
were \textbf{0.767} for Shared Ridge, \textbf{0.768} for Sensor-specific
Ridge, \textbf{0.166} for Period-routed Ridge, \textbf{0.428} for Raw
Attention, \textbf{0.219} for Raw Delay Mixer, \textbf{0.308} for
Attention + Decomposition, and \textbf{0.197} for the
Autoformer-inspired model. On the held-out Station 4 validation split,
the corresponding available RMSEs were \textbf{0.876, 0.173, 0.632,
0.336, 0.457, and 0.242}.

\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=3.69167in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image7.png}

Figure 4.1: Validation RMSE comparison for the four neural models and
linear baselines on established and held-out stations.

Delay mixing gave a larger improvement than decomposition alone.
Combining both gave the best neural result on both populations. The
horizon-wise results in Output 4.2 also show how these differences
change across the 48 forecast steps.

\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=2.11181in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image8.png}

Figure 4.2: Horizon-wise forecasting error across the 48-step prediction
horizon.

For deployment, I selected \textbf{Period-routed Ridge} for both
established stations and Station 4. It had the lowest validation RMSE
(\textbf{0.166} and \textbf{0.173}) while using only \textbf{59,904
parameters} and taking \textbf{0.013 s} to fit, compared with 373,026
parameters and 103.896 s for the Autoformer-inspired model. This makes
it the better practical choice despite the stronger neural model.

\hypertarget{data-processing-and-validation}{%
\subsubsection{2.1 Data Processing and
Validation}\label{data-processing-and-validation}}

The training data contains \textbf{43,656 observations}, while the
hidden test set contains \textbf{168 observations}. The external dataset
contains \textbf{43,824 rows and 10 features}, so these features are
also available for the full forecast period.

I used the target series together with the external features when
running the external-data version of the model. The target was
normalized using statistics from the training data. The normalized
target range in the training data was approximately \textbf{-1.08 to
9.86}.

For validation, I used a chronological split rather than randomly
shuffling the time series. The training portion contained \textbf{43,488
target observations}, followed by a validation horizon of \textbf{168
observations}, covering indices \textbf{43,489 to 43,656}. This keeps
the validation period after the training period and avoids using future
observations during training.

The model used a context length of \textbf{512 observations} and
predicted \textbf{168 points} at a time. This gives the model enough
history to capture the short-term temporal patterns while keeping the
forecast horizon equal to the required leaderboard horizon.

For the final forecast, I retrained using the full available target
history. The final training set contained \textbf{43,656 observed target
values}, and the model then predicted the hidden 168 points. The
submitted forecast therefore covers exactly indices \textbf{43,657
through 43,824}.

\hypertarget{autoformer-architecture}{%
\subsubsection{2.2 Autoformer
Architecture}\label{autoformer-architecture}}

I used an Autoformer-style model based on two main ideas: \textbf{series
decomposition} and \textbf{time-delay aggregation}. The input sequence
is first separated into a trend component and a residual/seasonal
component using moving-average decomposition. This lets the model handle
the slower changes in the series separately from the repeating patterns.

The main dependency mechanism is Auto-Correlation rather than standard
pointwise self-attention. The model compares the sequence at different
time delays and uses the strongest delays to aggregate information from
earlier parts of the sequence. This is useful for this dataset because
the target has strong short-lag correlation and clear periodic behavior.

The model uses a context of \textbf{512 time steps} and produces exactly
\textbf{168 forecast points}. External features can be provided
alongside the target sequence, and I trained both versions of the model:
one using only the target and one using the target together with the 10
external features.

The model was trained using chronological training and validation data.
I also used multiple random seeds (\textbf{42, 123, and 2026}) to check
whether the results were consistent across different initializations.

\hypertarget{training-setup-and-multi-seed-results}{%
\subsubsection{2.3 Training Setup and Multi-Seed
Results}\label{training-setup-and-multi-seed-results}}

I trained the model with three different random seeds: \textbf{42, 123,
and 2026}. The purpose was to check whether the results were strongly
dependent on the initial random initialization.

For the target-only version, the best validation RMSEs were
\textbf{83.55} for seed 42, \textbf{82.37} for seed 123, and
\textbf{81.07} for seed 2026. The external-data version performed
substantially better. Its best validation RMSEs were \textbf{52.48} for
seed 42, \textbf{36.75} for seed 123, and \textbf{39.13} for seed 2026.

These results show a clear benefit from including the external features
on the chronological validation split. The best external-data run was
seed 123, which reached an RMSE of \textbf{36.75} at epoch 16. The
original selected checkpoint from seed 42 reached a validation RMSE of
\textbf{52.48} at epoch 2.

The results also varied across seeds, so using a single run would not
give a complete picture of model performance. The multi-seed experiment
provides a better indication of how stable the model is.

\textbf{2.4 External-Data Ablation}

\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=4.02536in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image9.png}

I compared two versions of the model using the same chronological
validation setup: one using only the target history and one using the
target together with the \textbf{10 external features}.

The target-only model produced validation RMSEs between \textbf{81.07
and 83.55} across the three seeds. Adding the external features reduced
the validation RMSE to \textbf{52.48, 36.75, and 39.13} for seeds 42,
123, and 2026 respectively.

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.1042}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3770}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.5188}}@{}}
\toprule()
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Seed}
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Target only RMSE}
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{With external data RMSE}
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule()
\endhead
42 & 83.55 & 52.48 \\
123 & 82.37 & 36.75 \\
2026 & 81.07 & 39.13 \\
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

The improvement occurs for all three seeds, although the size of the
improvement varies. Seed 123 gives the best result with external data,
while seed 42 has the weakest external-data result. This suggests that
the external features contain useful information for forecasting the
target, but the model is still somewhat sensitive to its initialization.

Since the external variables are available for the full forecast period,
using them does not require access to the hidden target values.
Therefore, they can be used for the final 168-point forecast without
causing target leakage.

\hypertarget{final-training-and-forecast}{%
\subsubsection{2.5 Final Training and
Forecast}\label{final-training-and-forecast}}

\hypertarget{dlumsai651-deep-learning-for-space-time-and-graphsassignmentsdl4stg-pa1_prefinaldl4stg-pa1question-2---leaderboardreport_figurestask2_final_forecast.png}{%
\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\protect\includegraphics[width=6.5in,height=3.21216in]{vertopal_f570899ccaa646ef956901acfa15b964/media/image10.png}}{D:\textbackslash LUMS\textbackslash AI651 Deep Learning for space, time and graphs\textbackslash Assignments\textbackslash DL4STG-PA1\_prefinal\textbackslash DL4STG-PA1\textbackslash Question 2 - Leaderboard\textbackslash report\_figures\textbackslash task2\_final\_forecast.png}}\label{dlumsai651-deep-learning-for-space-time-and-graphsassignmentsdl4stg-pa1_prefinaldl4stg-pa1question-2---leaderboardreport_figurestask2_final_forecast.png}}

After comparing the models on the chronological validation split, I used
the external-data version for the final forecast. The final training run
used the full \textbf{43,656 observed target values} and the 10 external
features. This produced \textbf{42,977 training windows} with the
512-step context and 168-step prediction horizon.

The final model was trained for \textbf{16 epochs}. Its best validation
RMSE during the selected training run was \textbf{26.35} at epoch 16.
After training on the full available history, the model generated
exactly \textbf{168 predictions} for the hidden period.

The forecast starts at time index \textbf{43,657} and ends at
\textbf{43,824}, matching the required leaderboard range. The first five
predicted values were approximately \textbf{30.94, 5.58, 15.42, 11.66,
and 12.67}, while the last five were approximately \textbf{80.39, 79.36,
78.01, 71.49, and 63.71}.

\hypertarget{results-and-discussion}{%
\subsubsection{2.6 Results and
Discussion}\label{results-and-discussion}}

The main result from the validation experiments is that adding the
external features helped the model substantially. Across all three
seeds, the external-data model had a lower validation RMSE than the
target-only model. The best target-only RMSE was \textbf{81.07}, while
the best result with external data was \textbf{36.75}.

The different seeds did not give exactly the same results, which shows
that the training process has some sensitivity to initialization.
However, the improvement from using the external features was consistent
across all three seeds.

The final model was then trained using the complete observed target
history and the available external data. It produced the required
\textbf{168-point forecast} for indices \textbf{43657--43824}. The final
submission passed the checks for length, ordering, NaN values, and
infinite values.

Overall, the experiments suggest that the external variables provide
useful information that is not captured by the target history alone. The
decomposition and delay-based dependency mechanism also match the
structure of the data, which has strong short-lag correlation and
repeating patterns. The validation results were used to guide the final
model choice, while the hidden 168-point period was only used for the
final forecast.

